022292304784059525,-0.021283680573105812,-8.22540732769994e-06,-0.09686380624771118,-0.23728394508361816,-0.0006840273272246122,-3.015949550899677e-05,-5.125986263010418e-06,-2.145764938177308e-06,-2.3841855067985307e-07,-4.756337511935271e-05,-0.05502447858452797,-0.02107161656022072,-0.0002516191452741623,-0.16455389559268951,-2.276871418871451e-05,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-0.17959046363830566,-0.00021252757869660854,-0.22176435589790344,-2.6089367866516113,-0.002227446297183633,-0.0003219324571546167,-0.03435704857110977,-1.20026433467865,-0.0006447143969126046,-0.0009667966514825821,-0.030376702547073364,-0.05521908402442932,-0.10131146758794785,-0.007074781693518162,0.0,-0.08628445863723755,-2.3841855067985307e-07,-3.4689302992774174e-05,-8.344646857949556e-07,-3.576278118089249e-07,-2.622600959512056e-06,-3.2186455882765586e-06,-4.768370445162873e-07,-6.079655122448457e-06,-5.483612312673358e-06,-3.576278118089249e-07,-0.8459431529045105,-0.006179867312312126,-8.344646857949556e-07,0.0,-2.1457441107486375e-05,-0.0018047487828880548,-1.3947389561508317e-05,-2.264974000354414e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,9885,279,26313,979,279,47311,198,78045,79,2075,8,284,220,16,488,856,61,17,488,856,61,19,488,856,61,21,488,1124,67816,488,856,47822,17,17,11035,60,285,17779,553,279,47311,400,80,2075,8,284,220,16,488,856,488,856,61,17,488,856,61,18,488,1124,67816,488,856,47822,16,16,92,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the remainder when the polynomial\n\\[p(x) = 1 + x^2 + x^4 + x^6 + \\dots + x^{22}\\]is divided by the polynomial $q(x) = 1 + x + x^2 + x^3 + \\dots + x^{11}.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"cfe3669dd6c5d174cd66cdd4f216c5b416b485318f2f4e3e9e26e52d82b848bd"},"seconds":54.882216691970825,"seed":516956464567342921,"stop":"eos","text":"Let's tackle this problem step by step to find the remainder when the polynomial \\(p(x) = 1 + x^2 + x^4 + x^6 + \\dots + x^{22}\\) is divided by the polynomial \\(q(x) = 1 + x + x^2 + x^3 + \\dots + x^{11}\\).\n\n1. **Expressing \\(p(x)\\) and \\(q(x)\\):**\n\n   The polynomial \\(p(x)\\) is a geometric series of the form:\n   \\[\n   p(x) = \\sum_{k=0}^{11} x^{2k}\n   \\]\n   \n   The polynomial \\(q(x)\\) is also a geometric series:\n   \\[\n   q(x) = \\sum_{k=0}^{11} x^k\n   \\]\n\n2. **Using the geometric series sum formula:**\n\n   The sum of a geometric series \\(a + ar + ar^2 + \\cdots + ar^n\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=0}^{n} ar^k = a \\frac{r^{n+1} - 1}{r - 1}\n   \\]\n   \n   For \\(q(x)\\):\n   \\[\n   q(x) = \\frac{1 - x^{12}}{1 - x}\n   \\]\n   \n   For \\(p(x)\\):\n   \\[\n   p(x) = \\frac{1 - (x^2)^{12}}{1 - x^2} = \\frac{1 - x^{24}}{1 - x^2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Expressing \\(p(x)\\) in terms of \\(q(x)\\):**\n\n   We need to express \\(p(x)\\) as:\n   \\[\n   p(x) = q(x) \\cdot s(x) + r(x)\n   \\]\n   where \\(r(x)\\) is the remainder. Since the degree of \\(q(x)\\) is 11, the degree of \\(r(x)\\) must be less than 11.\n\n4. **Finding the remainder \\(r(x)\\):**\n\n   Given \\(q(x) = \\frac{1 - x^{12}}{1 - x}\\), we rewrite \\(p(x)\\) as:\n   \\[\n   p(x) = \\frac{1 - x^{24}}{1 - x^2}\n   \\]\n\n   To find \\(r(x)\\), we perform polynomial long division or use properties of roots and remainders for polynomials. Notice \\(x^{12} = 1\\) are the roots of \\(q(x)\\).\n\n5. **Evaluate \\(p(x)\\) at the roots of \\(q(x)\\):**\n\n   The roots of \\(q(x)\\) are the 12th roots of unity \\(\\omega^k\\) (where \\(\\omega = e^{2\\pi i /12}\\)). Evaluating \\(p(x)\\) at these roots:\n   \\[\n   p(\\omega^k) = 1 + (\\omega^k)^2 + (\\omega^k)^4 + \\cdots + (\\omega^k)^{22}\n   \\]\n   Since \\((\\omega^k)^{12} = 1\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   p(\\omega^k) = 1 + \\omega^{2k} + \\omega^{4k} + \\cdots + \\omega^{22k} = \\frac{1 - (\\omega^{2k})^{12}}{1 - \\omega^{2k}} = \\frac{1 - 1}{1 - \\omega^{2k}} = 0\n   \\]\n   This means \\(p(x)\\) is zero at each root of \\(q(x)\\), implying \\(p(x)\\) is exactly divisible by \\(q(x)\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion:**\n\n   Since \\(p(x)\\) is exactly divisible by \\(q(x)\\), the remainder \\(r(x)\\) is \\(0\\).\n\nTherefore, the remainder when \\(p(x)\\) is divided by \\(q(x)\\) is \\(\\boxed{0}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{0}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
